Stack snapshot
For orientation — this is bigger than a typical "product management" MVP already. See the scope note in the roadmap below.

Layer	Detail
Backend	Node.js + Express + TypeScript + Mongoose (MongoDB), deployed to Vercel as a serverless function
Frontend	React 18 + TypeScript + Vite, hand-rolled routing (no react-router), vanilla CSS with a token system
Auth	Custom scrypt password hashing + hand-rolled HMAC-SHA256 JWT (no bcrypt/jsonwebtoken library)
Modules built	Auth, Products, Categories, POS/Sales, Sales History, Purchases, Payments, Deliveries, Expenses, Stock Adjustments, Barcode Scanner, Dashboard
Tests	None found — no test files, no Jest/Vitest config, anywhere in the repo
Findings
Ordered by severity. file:line citations are real, from a direct read of the source — not generic advice.

Critical = blocks trusting the app with real data/money
High = fix before wider use
Medium = fix during MVP hardening
Low = backlog
Critical
Backend · Auth
backend/src/utils/auth.ts:18
JWT_SECRET falls back to the hardcoded string 'change-me-in-production' if the env var is unset. If it's ever missing in a deploy (Vercel env not configured), every session token becomes forgeable — anyone can mint a valid "owner" JWT.

Critical
Backend · Auth
backend/src/routes/authRoutes.ts
No rate limiting on /api/auth/login or /register (no express-rate-limit in dependencies). Login is fully exposed to credential brute-forcing.

Critical
Backend · Scripts
backend/src/scripts/seed.ts:26-28, createUser.ts:12-14
Demo owner credentials (admin@example.com / password123) are hardcoded with no NODE_ENV guard. Running the seed script against a real database creates a live, publicly-known admin account.

Critical
Backend · Testing
repo-wide
Zero automated test coverage — no test files, no Jest/Vitest config anywhere. Stock, COGS, and profit math (the core of what makes this trustworthy as an inventory tool) has no safety net against regressions.

Critical
Backend · Data
backend/.env.example:3; salesController.ts, purchasesController.ts
Every stock-affecting write (sales, purchases, adjustments, deliveries) uses Mongoose sessions/transactions, which require a MongoDB replica set. The shipped .env.example points at a plain standalone mongodb://localhost:27017/... — against that config, every one of these writes throws.

Critical
Backend · Logic
dashboardController.ts vs. salesController.ts
The dashboard's "Total Revenue" is computed from the Payment collection (paymentStatus:'completed'), while sales revenue lives independently on Sale.grossRevenue. The two are never reconciled — the app's headline metric can silently disagree with actual recorded sales.

High
Frontend · Auth
frontend/src/api/api.ts:9
JWT is stored in localStorage, readable by any injected script — no httpOnly cookie option. Standard XSS-exfiltration exposure.

High
Frontend · Auth
frontend/src/api (no response.use found)
No axios response interceptor for 401s exists anywhere. An expired/invalid token just surfaces as a generic "failed to load" error on whatever page the user is on — no uniform redirect to login.

High
Frontend · Structure
frontend/src/components, layouts (empty .gitkeep only)
Zero shared UI components exist — every page hand-rolls its own table/loading/error markup. This is the root cause of the loading-state inconsistency across Dashboard/Expenses/Stock Adjustments (fixed on only 3 of ~8 pages so far) — the same fix has to be hand-applied per page instead of once.

High
Backend · Errors
backend/src/app.ts:53-56
Global error handler returns err.message directly to the client with no NODE_ENV gating — internal Mongoose/DB error text leaks to callers.

High
Backend · Security
backend/package.json (deps)
No helmet — the API ships with no security headers (CSP, X-Content-Type-Options, etc.).

High
Backend · Validation
paymentsController.ts:14,19; deliveriesController.ts:44
Mass-assignment: Payment.create(req.body) and similar update calls pass the raw request body straight into Mongoose with no field allow-list — a caller can set paymentStatus directly to "completed" on creation, bypassing any intended workflow.

High
Backend · Auth
backend/src/utils/auth.ts:70
JWT signature check uses plain !== string comparison instead of crypto.timingSafeEqual (unlike the password check just above it, which does this correctly) — a minor timing side-channel in otherwise-solid hand-rolled crypto.

Medium
Backend · Validation
repo-wide controllers
No validation library (zod/joi/express-validator) anywhere — request validation is ad hoc if checks of inconsistent depth per controller.

Medium
Backend · Data
Product, Sale, Purchase models
Money (price, costPrice, amount, grandTotal) is stored as plain floating-point Number everywhere rather than integer minor-units or Decimal128 — rounding error can accumulate across line items.

Medium
Backend · Data
backend/src/models/Payment.ts
Payment has no real relation to Sale — only a free-text orderId string. No referential integrity between a sale and its payments.

Medium
Backend · Perf
Product.category/status; Purchase/StockAdjustment.createdAt
Missing indexes on fields that are filtered/sorted on constantly — fine at demo scale, will show up as real latency as inventory/history grows.

Medium
Backend · Duplication
sales/purchases/stockAdjustments/expenses/productsController.ts
Pagination boilerplate (page/limit clamping + Promise.all([find, count])) is copy-pasted across 5 controllers with no shared helper.

Medium
Frontend · Forms
ProductManagementPage.tsx:102-118
Product create/edit form has no client-side numeric/negative-number guard on price/cost/stock — relies only on HTML min="0", which doesn't stop a typed negative or a pasted non-numeric value (NaN) from reaching the API.

Medium
Frontend · Routing
App.tsx:16-29, 42-47; BarcodeScannerPage.tsx:193-217
Two parallel navigation mechanisms coexist: App.tsx's navigate(), and the scanner page manually calling history.pushState + dispatching a synthetic popstate. Unmapped/unknown paths also silently fall back to the dashboard instead of a real 404.

Medium
Frontend · Duplication
15+ pages
The exact expression err.response?.data?.error || err.message || fallback is copy-pasted 15+ times across pages instead of one shared getErrorMessage() helper.

Medium
Frontend · A11y
ProductManagementPage.tsx:268,274; SalesPage.tsx:205-215; PurchasesPage.tsx:222-227
Icon-only edit/delete/remove-item buttons mostly lack aria-label — only 2 exist in the whole app. title alone isn't reliably announced by screen readers.

Medium
Frontend · Process
styles.css:388,431 · git log
Confirmed in the commit history: three consecutive commits (2a11829 → 33a7d40 → 3abfc0d → 9dd84fd) oscillated the same filter min-height/border-radius value back and forth, reaching for !important instead of the CSS custom-property token system that already exists in styles.css:1-21. Styling is being patched by trial-and-error rather than through the tokens already in place.

Medium
Frontend · Build
frontend/src/api/api.ts:4
VITE_API_BASE_URL silently falls back to http://localhost:5000/api with no build-time enforcement — a production build with a missing env var would ship silently pointed at localhost.

Medium
Frontend · Dashboard
DashboardPage.tsx:38,42,54
Three independent dashboard fetches all write errors into the same single error state variable — whichever request fails last silently overwrites any earlier error, so a real failure can go unreported.

Low
Backend · Data
categoriesController.ts, Category.ts:19
No cycle-check on category parent references — a category could in theory be set as its own ancestor.

Low
Backend · Cleanup
src/services, src/middleware (.gitkeep only); src/index.ts
Empty placeholder folders and a vestigial "legacy entrypoint" src/index.ts alongside server.ts and api/index.ts — dead structure that adds confusion for no benefit.

Low
Frontend · Cleanup
frontend/src/api/client.ts
A near-pointless 3-line re-export of api.ts that only adds indirection — nothing imports it distinctly.

Low
Frontend · Types
21 occurrences, mostly `catch (err: any)`
21 uses of : any undermine tsconfig.json's strict: true, concentrated in error-handling catch blocks.

Low
Backend · Deploy
backend/src/config/db.ts
Serverless Mongo connection caching has no bufferCommands:false or explicit readiness handling — possible hanging requests during cold-start connection storms at scale.

Getting to an MVP
Scope reality check: this is already a full retail ERP — Products, Categories, POS, Purchases, Payments, Deliveries, Expenses, Stock Adjustments, a barcode scanner, and dashboard analytics all exist and mostly work. That's past MVP feature-wise. The actual gap to a trustworthy MVP isn't "build more" — it's fixing the 6 critical issues above so the data and money it tracks can be trusted, then tightening the frontend structure so new pages stop repeating the same fixable bugs. The roadmap below treats "MVP" as: safe to hand to a real shop owner, not safe to leave running unattended forever.
Phase 0 — Stop the bleeding
~1–2 days
These are the issues that make the app unsafe to trust with real inventory or money today. Nothing else matters until these are closed.

Make the server refuse to boot if JWT_SECRET is unset or equals the default string
Add express-rate-limit to /api/auth/login and /register
Guard seed.ts/createUser.ts behind a NODE_ENV !== 'production' check plus an explicit confirm flag
Pick one canonical source for "Total Revenue" (Sale or Payment) and reconcile the dashboard against it
Document/enforce the MongoDB replica-set requirement (Atlas free tier qualifies); fail fast with a clear error if the connected instance can't run transactions
Gate error-handler detail behind NODE_ENV so production never leaks raw DB error text
Phase 1 — Lock the MVP scope
~0.5 day, decision work
A decision, not code. Confirms what ships in v1 vs. what's deferred, so Phases 2–4 aren't spent hardening modules that won't launch yet.

Core loop to ship: Auth → Categories → Products/Inventory → Sales (POS) → Dashboard
Defer-candidates worth a deliberate yes/no: Payments as a module separate from Sales, Deliveries, Stock Adjustments as its own page vs. folding into Purchases
Barcode scanner is a real differentiator and already reasonably solid — keep it in scope
Phase 2 — Fix the frontend at the root
~2–3 days
The loading-state inconsistency, the 15+ duplicated error-parsing lines, and the missing 401 handling all trace back to one cause: no shared components. Fixing that stops the same bug from needing to be fixed N times.

Extract shared Table, LoadingState/EmptyState, ErrorBanner, StatusPill components into the currently-empty components/ folder
Add an axios response interceptor: any 401 → clear auth state, redirect to /login, everywhere, uniformly
Centralize getErrorMessage(err), replace the 15+ duplicated inline versions
Apply the shared components to Dashboard/Expenses/Stock Adjustments to close the loading-state gap the other 3 pages already have
Add aria-label to icon-only action buttons while touching these components anyway
Phase 3 — Data integrity & validation
~2 days
Closes the mass-assignment and ad-hoc-validation gaps so the API can't be pushed into an inconsistent state by a malformed or malicious request.

Introduce zod schemas per resource on the backend; reuse the shape for frontend form validation where practical
Add explicit field allow-lists on Payment/Delivery/Product write endpoints
Add the missing indexes (Product.category/status, Purchase/StockAdjustment.createdAt)
Add a category-parent cycle check
Phase 4 — Test safety net
~2–3 days
Without this, every future change to stock/COGS/profit logic is a guess. This is the highest-leverage phase for long-term velocity.

Backend: Vitest/Jest + Supertest, using mongodb-memory-server (replica-set mode) so transaction-dependent tests actually run in CI
Priority coverage: auth (hash/verify/JWT round-trip), stock decrement/increment races, COGS and profit math, the sale/purchase transaction rollback path
Frontend: smoke tests for the POS checkout flow and product CRUD, at minimum
Phase 5 — Polish & ship
~1–2 days
Cleanup and one deliberately-deferred item that doesn't need to block launch.

Remove dead code: src/index.ts, api/client.ts, empty .gitkeep-only folders
Fail the production build if VITE_API_BASE_URL is unset
Defer to v1.1: migrating money fields from float Number to Decimal128/integer minor-units — real fix, but a bigger, riskier lift than anything blocking launch
Findings compiled from a direct read of every file under backend/src and frontend/src, plus the last 24 commits. Estimates assume one developer familiar with the codebase; parallelizing backend/frontend work compresses the total to roughly 6–7 focused days.